C++20 <map>: operator[] insert + access.

Reclassified CORE -> KNOWNBUG by the member-typedef conformance fix
([temp.inst]/1-2, [dcl.typedef]).  Previously this passed only because the
non-conformant *truncated* std::map had its operations havoc'd (symex executed
none of the real container; the property was vacuous).  The fix makes the front
end conformant and symex now executes the REAL std::map / _Rb_tree machinery.

Current layer (2026-07-15 evening): this exact program verifies fully
UNBOUNDED under --cpp11 after the delegating-piecewise-constructor chain
was fixed (cpp11_piecewise_delegating_ctor, cpp11_static_cast_base_rref,
and the earlier cpp11_* piecewise tests are all CORE, and
'm[1]=42; assert(m[1]==42)' verifies in ~1.6s bounded).  Under --cpp20
the headers construct the node through a DIFFERENT chain
(std::construct_at / _S_construct, C++20 constexpr allocator_traits):
the stored key byte is again malloc garbage (trace: storage[0]=64), so
the read-back re-inserts and the unbounded tree walk diverges in the
_Rb_tree_decrement model.  NEXT: peel the C++20 construct_at chain the
same way.  The FRONT-END chain is now complete: the free-function-template
new-initializer pack expansion (cpp11_construct_at_pack_args), the void
constexpr wrapper deferral (cpp11_void_constexpr_wrapper_defer), and the
trailing-return decltype-over-pack-new deduction
(cpp11_construct_at_decltype_return) are all fixed and CORE.  With them,
--cpp20 instantiates std::construct_at and runs std::map's piecewise node
construction (the key is stored correctly).  The REMAINING reason this
test does not pass is a bounded-model-checking SOLVER-SCALING limit on the
fully-inlined C++20 container: without an unwind bound the _Rb_tree
machinery builds a formula the SAT backend cannot discharge in the time
budget (at --unwind 5 the equation is built in ~6.5min and the solver
then runs out of resources).  This is not a front-end conformance issue:
the identical program verifies unbounded under --cpp11 (the C++11 headers
inline less).  Reclassify to CORE if/when the container machinery is
made tractable (a lighter model, or bounded --unwind with a coarser
property).

Second-tier verification-PERFORMANCE item, not a conformance one; the previous
"pass" was unsound.  Reclassify back to CORE once symex is tractable on the real
container.
See doc/architectural/cpp-class-template-instantiation-plan.md.

--paths lifo 2026-07-17: path-wise symex NEUTRALIZES the one-shot
blow-up completely -- a verdict in ~42-60s at --unwind 5/6/7 (vs. >15
min without a verdict one-shot).  BUT the verdict is VERIFICATION
FAILED, and the counterexample REFRAMES this test: the failing
dereferences are inside std::_Rb_tree_insert_and_rebalance, which is
defined in libstdc++'s COMPILED tree.cc (not the headers), so CBMC has
no body for it and havocs the call: the tree linkage after insert is
nondet garbage.  m[1] == 42 can therefore never verify, at ANY unwind,
with ANY solver.  The real blocker is a missing model for the tree.cc
entry points (_Rb_tree_insert_and_rebalance, _Rb_tree_increment,
_Rb_tree_decrement, _Rb_tree_rebalance_for_erase) -- e.g. in
src/cpp/library/ next to new.c.  A model may do plain BST linking +
header (leftmost/rightmost) maintenance and skip the rebalancing
rotations: recoloring affects only balance, not the container's
functional semantics.  Once modelled, --paths lifo (or one-shot at
--unwind 5) is expected to be tractable.

CORRECTION 2026-07-17 (follow-up): the tree.cc entry points ARE already
modelled (cpp_typecheck_stdlib.cpp: make_rb_insert_and_rebalance_body,
_Rb_tree_increment/_Rb_tree_decrement successor/predecessor walks,
make_rb_rebalance_for_erase_body) and the model bodies ARE present in
the goto program -- the earlier "havoc'd" reading misread the model's
own parameter ASSUMEs.  The real remaining blocker, isolated from the
--paths lifo counterexample down to a 6-line reproducer
(cpp20_pair_converting_ctor): C++20 libstdc++ pair's requires-clause +
explicit(bool) converting constructor pair(U1&&, U2&&) is instantiated
WITHOUT a body (constexpr/macro-flagged, nil value, never queued for
deferred conversion), so _M_get_insert_unique_pos's `_Res(__y, 0)`
returns garbage and insert misbehaves.  Fix that defect first; then
retest one-shot and --paths lifo.

UPDATE 2026-07-17 night: the pair converting-constructor layer is FIXED
(cpp20_pair_converting_ctor and both requires-atom tests are CORE).
NEW FRONTIER exposed one level up: _M_get_insert_hint_unique_pos's
`__res` (a C++20 pair<_Base_ptr,_Base_ptr>, which now has constrained
constructors and derives from the empty __pair_base) receives GARBAGE
through the sret-lowered tail call `return _M_get_insert_unique_pos(k)`
-- the inner call's result {NULL, &header} is correct in ITS __res, but
the value is lost in the hidden-result handoff (trace: first=NULL+
177253069).  Suspect the elide_cpp_returned_temporaries substitution
interacting with the derived-to-__pair_base "ignoring typecast" shape
in the returned-temporary construction.  Then the garbage position
flows into _Rb_tree_insert_and_rebalance (__p->_M_left dereference
failures).
